ion Applies Segment Anything Model for Responding to Flood Emergencies\nL150: \nL151: The Universities Space Research Association and Meta are collaborating to help support water observing systems set up by the U.S. Geological Survey.\nL152: \nL153: December 18, 2025\nL154: \nL155: cite28†Learn More L156: \nL157: Blog\nL158: \nL159: December 16, 2025\nL160: \nL161: #### Computer Vision\nL162: #### Introducing SAM Audio: The First Unified Multimodal Model for Audio Separation\nL163: \nL164: SAM Audio transforms audio processing by making it easy to isolate any sound from complex audio mixtures using natural, multimodal prompts — whether through text, visual cues, or marking time segments.\nL165: \nL166: December 16, 2025\nL167: \nL168: cite29†Learn More L169: \nL170: Blog\nL171: \nL172: November 19, 2025\nL173: \nL174: #### Computer Vision\nL175: #### Introducing SAM 3D: Powerful 3D Reconstruction for Physical World Images\nL176: \nL177: This release introduces two new state-of-the-art models: SAM 3D Objects for object and scene reconstruction, and SAM 3D Body for human body and shape estimation.\nL178: \nL179: November 19, 2025\nL180: \nL181: cite30†Learn More L182: \nL183: Blog\nL184: \nL185: November 24, 2025\nL186: \nL187: #### Computer Vision\nL188: #### How Conservation X Labs Is Using Segment Anything Model 3 for Endangered Wildlife Monitoring\nL189: \nL190: Start-ups like Conservation X Labs are using Meta’s Segment Anything Models to bolster on-the-ground conservation expertise.\nL191: \nL192: November 24, 2025\nL193: \nL194: cite31†Learn More L195: \nL196: Blog\nL197: \nL198: November 21, 2025\nL199: \nL200: #### Open Source\nL201: #### ExecuTorch Adoption in Reality Labs: Powering On-Device AI Across Meta Devices\nL202: \nL203: ExecuTorch, Meta’s open source, lightweight, and efficient inference engine, has been instrumental in enabling on-device AI capabilities across Meta’s family of apps.\nL204: \nL205: November 21, 2025\nL206: \nL207: cite